\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST-Australian Business News - June 8. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,132 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 1998\par 02:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Compiled for Reuters by Media Monitors) \par \par \uc2  THE AUSTRALIAN \par \par \uc2  Economists still expect Reserve Bank of Australia governor Ian Macfarlane to try to support the value of the Australian dollar, which has reached a new 12-year low and is expected to fall below US60 cents, through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markes rather than risk placing the Australian economy into recession by increasing interest rates. Page 33. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Westpac institutional banking head David Morgan said the cost savings of closing the bank's Tokyo, New York and London back offices had not yet been calculated, but revealed the centralisation of its global back office functions in Sydney had provided a A$20 million bottom-line advantage in the six months to the end of March, with A$40 million in recurring annual savings expected from the rationalisation. Page 35. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  The American expansion plans of major Australian gold producer Normandy Mining  were thrown into chaos yesterday when a Peruvian court dismissed the company's claim over a 12.35 per cent stake in the 24 million ounce Yanacocha mine, the subject of a three-year legal battle. Page 35. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Insurance and motoring organisation NRMA has again be plunged into management turmoil following the resignation of chief executive Malcolm Jones after only two years at the helm. NRMA president and former chief executive contender Nicholas Whitlam said on Friday Jones had resigned to pursue other interests. Page 35. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Listed tollway operator Hills Motorway  has reported that traffic volumes in its first full-year of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  were 30 per cent below the 72,000 vehicles per day predicted in the company's prospectus. Hills Motorway general manager Bill Clark said there had been compound monthly growth of about 3 per cent since opening, and predicted further improvement into next year. Page 35. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Harsh trading conditions in New Zealand and a late Western Australian season have been blamed as the main reasons for a 52 per cent fall in rural retailer Iama's  earnings in the six months to March 31 to A$3.3 million, compared with A$6.2 million in the pevious corresponding period. Iama still expects to post a full-year result of A$16.1 million in light of good rains since March. Page 35. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  THE SYDNEY MORNING HERALD \par \par \uc2  Economists expect the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Australian dollar after international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading resumes, but there is concern the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may find itself ineffectual when faced with major overseas investors trying to cash in on the Reserve Bank's efforts to support the sliding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Overseas traders will lead the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing with a public holiday in Australia today. Page 33. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stockbroker CS First Boston has declared the A$1 billion New South Wales TAB\tab     float a "safe bet", but has warned there is only limited opportunity for growth because the company's new gaming products are faced with an already crowded market, and has recommended Victoria's TABCorp  as a better investment. Page 33.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An Australian Securities Commission April Fool's Day joke, in which the organisation advertised for investors to put money into schemes including a blue bottle farm, an air and land package that allows investors to charge for use of their airspace, and a Geep" farm, has caught out 700 people. The unexpected response prompted the Commission to use investors' inquiries to give them information about sham investment schemes. Page 33.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par AMP\tab     is today expected to finalise the numbers of policyholders who have chosen to take advantage of the share facility to sell their allocations before next week's listing, with some estimates putting the take up as high as 15 per cent. AMP will then annonce how many shares will be available to institutions, which are expected to generate unprecedented levels of trading in the first few weeks after the insurer lists. Page 33.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brierley Investments  founder Ron Brierley yesterday dismissed speculation the company's 25 per cent stake in Australian publisher John Fairfax Holdings  was up for sale, but labelled the company's assumption of chairmanship of the company, which it ceded rcently, a "mistake". Page 35.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par M2 listed operator Hills has announced a May recovery in traffic volumes on the Sydney tollway, with average traffic reaching 56,298 vehicles a day, a 4.3 per cent increase on April's figures. Hills is expected to decide within the next fortnight whether o pay a distribution to investors, but traffic levels are still well below the prospectus forecast of 72,000. Page 35.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THE AGE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par National Australia Bank chief economist Alan Oster has forecast a probable downgrade of the bank's prediction of 2.5 per cent economic growth for 1998-99 in light of the plummeting Australian dollar, claiming anecdotal evidence from businesses was "not good". He said a worsening of the Asian economic crisis and a domestic slowdown could result in rising interest rates, which would then be cut after Christmas to boost the economy. Page B1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Basslink Development Corporation chairman Michael Vertigan has announced that the Tasmanian Government-sponsored organisation will call for expressions of interest from private companies to construct, own and operate an electricity transmission pipeline btween Tasmania and Victoria. Page B1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Soon-to-be-listed insurer AMP is expected to announce today how many policyholders have chosen to sell their share allocations through the company's facility, which closed last Wednesday, with speculation up to 15 per cent of the new shareholders will Cash in on their windfall. AMP is also expected to announce this week whether it will issue additional shares for institutions. Page B1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par About 700 would-be investors responded to joke investment scheme advertisements which promised high returns, including a blue bottle jellyfish farm, placed in the media by the Australian Securities Commission since April Fool's Day, prompting the Commissin to turn the joke into an information campaign on high-risk and sham investments. Page B1.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Federal Government has been forced during a Senate estimates committee hearing to reveal its will spend about A$35 million on preparing for the introduction of its tax reform package, before details of the plan have been released. About A$10 million wll be spent on an education and advertising campaign, but such a campaign would be halted if an election was called. Page B2.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brierley Investments founder president Ron Brierley has rejected speculation the investment company is planning to sell its 23.9 per cent Fairfax stake, but did not rule out a possible sale in the future. Speculation about the stake has come amid a boardrom shakeout that has seen five directors leave in the past six weeks. Page B3.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Sydney Newsroom - 61-2 9373-1800, sydney.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6803e0y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
