\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr/Late -2: Mkt Wary Of Move Under US$0.6000 \b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 1998\par 09:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Analysts have cited a host of negative factors facing the Australian dollar, including soft gold and commodity prices, an expected deterioration of Australia's current-account deficit, a generally weak yen and lingering speculation about a further cut in domestic interest rates. \par \par \uc2  A few dealers also remarked that the Australian dollar is being used as a "surrogate" for the Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The illiquid nature of these Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has seen investors looking to hedge their Asian exposures by selling in the more liquid Australian dollar markets, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  According to traders, U.S. speculative players, such as hedge funds, have been the key sellers of the Australian dollar of late, although Asian sellers have also been sighted. \par \par \uc2  Moving into the overnight session, most traders agree that the Australian dollar is headed for lower levels, despite the threat of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Michael Workman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commonwealth Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ltd., said that despite retaining "an oversold nature", Australian dollar risk remains weighted to the downside. He added that "you don't stand in the way of capital," pointing to the aggressive flow of funds out of Australia this week. \par \par \uc2  Workman said a sustained break of US$0.6050 targets US$0.6010. He added that a break of the latter would be a particularly bearish signal, with traders citing few key levels to target then, ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's all-time low at US$0.5717 reached in July 1986. \par \par \uc2  Colonial State Bank chief economic Craig James said that "while the lower Australian dollar is a consequence of the Asian crisis, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also a victim of the benign negligence of Group of Seven powers to stabilize the (Asia) crisis." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par According to James, "Continued inaction (by the G-7) may lead to a\par second wave of the crisis, further falls in commodity prices and, as a\par result, the Australian dollar testing US$0.6000."\par    -By Sandra Muldoon; 612-9290-8871; smuldoon@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 05-06-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0733GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6504tor\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
