\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS-Aussie dlr vols spike on approach to $0.60. \b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 1998\par 13:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 5 (Reuters) - Implied volatilities for Australian dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options spiked up across the curve as the Aussie dollar crumpled towards a key 12-year low and psychological barrier at US$0.60 on Friday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options dealers said some option-related selling of U.S. dollars from US$0.60 strike-holders appeared in the market, but that it seemed to have even less of an uplifting effect on spot than the repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There has been some activity on the barrier market, but the barrier levels are not as important as the fact the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in the market," said one options salesperson at a U.S. investment bank in London. \par \par \uc2  She said longer-dated options in the one-year area and beyond have been enjoying strong interest as investors climb out of short-dated and move to the long end of the Aussie vols curve. \par \par \uc2  "We have had loads and loads of one-year and two-year and even longer-dated volatility options buyers," she said. \par \par \uc2  Australian dollar vols spiked up to 12.50/13.50 percent in the one-week short-dated area from 12.00/13.00 in late European trading on Thursday as the Australian dollar set a fresh 12-year low at $0.6010. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) was forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in Asia on Thursday to rescue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a who's who of U.S. investment banks and hedge funds dumped the Aussie dollar in massive amounts. \par \par \uc2  But the spending spree, which dealers estimated could have been as high as A$3.0 billion, bought only a brief breathing space as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly notched down in European trading hours. \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark vols were subdued after the Bank of England raised official interest rates by a quarter point to 7.50 percent on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said sterling vols rose in the short end and fell in the long end of the curve as sterling bears came out in force in the belief this latest move was the end of British rate rises for the current economic cycle. \par \par \uc2  "The stuff was beginning to be sold off as it is and if anything people were just bidding the risk/reversals more for the sterling puts," said Derek Samman, head options trader at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London. "Everyone figures this is clearly the last time rates will do anything other than go down." \par \par \uc2  Sterling/mark vols rose in the short end of the curve, with one-month sterling/mark vols edging up to 7.70/8.10 from 7.50/8.00 on Thursday and one-year vols slipped to 7.50/7.80 from 7.60/7.80. \par \par \uc2  Implied volatilities for dollar-paired options were sidelined on Friday by the action in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as a result of the routine Friday slowdown ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen vols slipped to 12.10/12.50 from 12.20/12.70 in European trading on Thursday, while six-month vols fell to 13.10/13.40 from 13.20/13.70. One-year vols rose to 13.50/13.90 from 13.30/13.80. One-month risk/reversals remained in favour of yen calls at -0.4/+0.7. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/mark vols slid to 8.65/8.85 from 8.90/9.30 on Thursday in Europe, while six-month vols eased to 9.30/9.50 from 9.40/9.70 and one-year vols squeaked lower to 9.45/9.65 from 9.50/9.70. One-month risk/reversals remained in favour of mark calls at -0.4/+0.7. ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, uk.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6503jag\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
