\par\par \b \uc2  World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -3:  \highlight22\uc2 Australia Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again \b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 1998\par 00:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The market is rife with speculation that Russia will need emergency aid from the International Monetary Fund and G-7 nations to help resolve its latest financial crisis, despite a successful and unexpected issuance of $1.25 billion in global bonds Wednesday, which helped stabilize the ruble and Russia's stock market. \par \par \uc2  But Russian Prime Minister Sergei Kiriyenko dismissed the possibility of such steps, saying Thursday that Russia doesn't need any foreign government aid. Kiriyenko did, however, float a request to creditors for the possibility of restructuring Russia's foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  The dollar for next-day delivery closed at 6.1780-6.1810 rubles (RUR), up from RUR6.1600-6.1700 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Australian dollar dropped to a new 12-year low, but repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of Australian appeared to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At around 2130 GMT, early Friday morning in Australia, the RBA again bought Australian dollars, this time at US$0.6090, the most recent in a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past 15 hours, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. \par \par \uc2  A trader with a Sydney-based bank who declined to be named said the buying again appeared "very serious." \par \par \uc2  "Given the price action over last 24 hours, the defined range between US$0.6080-0.6155 is likely to hold into the weekend, but the bias is still lower because none of the fundamentals have changed," said Todd Keynes, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk management at Commonwealth Bank of Australia in New York. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the RBA bought the Australian dollar several times - including through the U.S. Federal Reserve midmorning in New York trading - in an attempt to halt the sell-off. In response, the Australian dollar rose to an intraday high of US$US0.6154, before retreating to US$0.6113 late in New York, down from US$0.6163 late Wednesday in Sydney. \par \par \uc2  Traders have attributed the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall to the weakness of the Japanese yen, Asia's economic troubles and declining global commodity prices. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, the krona (SEK) barely budged after the Riksbank, Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said it will cut its securities repurchase rate to 4.10% from 4.35%, effective June 10, and left open the prospect for further rate cuts over the near term. Late in Europe, the mark was quoted at SEK4.3835, unchanged from Wednesday \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 04-06-98 \par \par \uc2  2204GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6404t3k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
