\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Bond Prices Seen Continuing Overnight Price Falls \b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 1998\par 00:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones Australia and New Zealand Report \par \uc2 DJAN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--Prices of Australian bonds will likely open lower Friday in line with weaker domestic bond futures contracts and U.S. Treasurys overnight and a still ailing Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Prices of U.S. Treasurys fell in U.S. trading Thursday. In New York, benchmark 10-year Treasury notes closed yielding 5.58%, up from 5.54% Wednesday and when Australian bonds closed Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In computerized overnight trading in Australia, June 10-year bond futures finished at 94.545, down 0.060, while three-year bond contracts closed at 94.96, down 0.08. September bank bill futures ended at 95.02, down 0.05. September bill futures aren't the front-month contract but are the most heavily traded. \par \par \uc2  The biggest price move overnight was the three-year bond contract. That traded as low as 94.95 on comments from Reserve Bank of Australia governor Ian MacFarlane, said a trader at a European securities house. \par \par \uc2  Speaking at a luncheon of Australian business leaders in London overnight, MacFarlane said he has been "somewhat surprised" by the tendency of Australian financial markets to price in an easing of official interest rates into short-term money market instruments. "I would have thought that was a very unlikely outcome," he said, referring to a rate cut. And later during a question-and-answer session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said "there's not a strong case for a reduction in interest rates." \par \par \uc2  The trader predicted there would be further price losses during Friday's trading session, based on the view U.S.-based managed funds, known as commodity trading advisors, still have positions to liquidate. \par \par \uc2  "Why would anyone want to be long," Australian bonds, he asked. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defence of the Australian dollar. Its latest buying, at US$0.6090, was described by one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader as "very serious." The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trading at US$0.6095 now. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moody's Investors Service has put New Zealand's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par credit ratings under review for possible downgrade, citing a concerns\par about the "sharp deterioration in the country's external accounts."\par Moody's said its review will focus on a number of factors including\par developments in Asia, the strength of the domestic sector, the country's\par ability to increase savings and how economic policy will facilitate an\par orderly return to a "more sustainable" external account position.\par -By Marion Williams, 61-2-9290-8874, mswilliams@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 04-06-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2208GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  22765 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djan000020010916du64001gw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
