\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr Weaker Early; Yen, Gold, Commodities Down \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 1998\par 00:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The Australian dollar is weaker early Wednesday, in line with a soft yen, and a drop in gold and commodity prices overnight. \par \par \uc2  At 2200 GMT, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is quoted at US$0.6208, down from US$0.6238 late in Sydney Tuesday. It also is at Y85.55, below Y85.84 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar slid gradually lower overnight before bottoming at US$0.6200 in early trading Wednesday. Behind the slide was ongoing yen weakness, and a 3.21 points fall in the KR-CRB index of commodity prices to 215.92. Also, gold fell overnight, the metal last quoted at US$295.55/oz, down US$4.05/oz from late Sydney trading. \par \par \uc2  Factors weighing on the yen, according to dealers, include a pessimistic outlook on the Japanese economy, nervousness about unsteady South Korean markets, and damped speculation that the Bank of Japan will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also cite the weekend U.S. News and World report that suggested U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin was prepared to see the U.S. dollar climb to Y140.0, even Y150.0, if it would help the Japanese economy. However, Rubin Tuesday expressed "surprise" at the weekend report and reiterated concern about the weak yen, which prompted a short-lived bounce by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight. \par \par \uc2  According to one dealer, U.S. and International Money Market sellers were notable during the Australian dollar's overnight decline. He says that while the yen remains under pressure, and commodity prices weak, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue to probe lower levels. The dealer is looking for a retest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent low at US$0.6173 over coming days. \par \par \uc2  However, likely to provide some support will be nervousness about possible further Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Australian dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dive to a near 12-year low at US$0.6173. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the domestic economic front Wednesday, the Department of Industrial\par Relations will release its first quarter wage trends in enterprise\par bargaining report at 0130GMT. However, the data aren't likely to have\par much impact on the Australian dollar.\par    -By Sandra Muldoon; 612-9290-8871; smuldoon@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  26-05-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2213GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1054 22766 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010914du5q00jyt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
