\par\par \b \uc2  Australian dlr slide weakens rate cut outlook-poll. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Victoria Thieberger \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 1998\par 03:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, May 19 (Reuters) - The rapid slide of the Australian dollar and its inflationary implications has pushed back expectations of a cut in official interest rates, a Reuters poll has showed. \par \par \uc2  The poll of 24 economists, conducted last Friday, found the average chance of an easing in monetary policy declined to 29 percent from 33.7 percent in a poll conducted two weeks previously. The median remains unchanged at 30 percent. Several analysts pushed back the expected timing of a cut to the fourth quarter. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen five percent since the start of the month to fresh 12-year lows as the turmoil in Indonesia worsened, prompting rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Tuesday morning to hoist the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back above 62 U.S. cents. By 11.55 a.m. (0155 GMT), it was trading at 62.44 cents. \par \par \uc2  "Easing now would risk seeing a precipitous fall in the Aussie dollar," said AMP Society chief economist Shane Oliver. \par \par \uc2  "Recent economic data has been volatile and the bank would want to get a better handle on it over the next few months," said Oliver, who puts the chance of a rate cut at around 60 percent but has pushed back his expected timing to September or October, after the next round of inflation and wages figures. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar's sharp decline on the back of renewed Indonesian strife is seen as ruling out an official rate cut in the near term on several fronts. \par \par \uc2  First, the RBA would not want to risk a further slump in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by cutting rates now. And second, the easing in monetary conditions implied by a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly since exporters receive the greatest benefit of a soft dollar, means the decline has done some of the RBA's work for it. The weak dollar also pushes up import prices, which are the only source of inflationary pressure on the horizon. \par \par \uc2  Concerns about the strength of the domestic economy have eased somewhat since April employment and March retail sales and building approvals figures all showed a healthy bounceback after recent soft readings, and RBA governor Ian Macfarlane himself greeted news of the 58,200 rise in jobs as "a relief". \par \par \uc2  But some economists still believe a rate cut is needed in the short-term as insurance for the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  "The impact from Asia has already been enough to require further stimulus," said Colonial State Bank economist Richard Grace, who believes policy could be eased in a few months from now once the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilises. \par \par \uc2  "They would not want to do anything which would trigger a further fall now," he said. \par \par \uc2  The RBA governor said in his recent semi-annual testimony to a parliamentary committee that the RBA board had seriously considered an easing at its most recent monthly meeting, and signalled the Reserve Bank has moved to an easing bias. \par \par \uc2  But the fragile dollar and the delivery of the federal budget have temporarily closed the window of opportunity for a rate cut, according to those who still believe a cut is needed. \par \par \uc2  "I am getting more and more concerned about overall GDP growth in the next 12 months," said Citibank Australia chief economist Stephen Koukoulas. \par \par \uc2  But he believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have "missed the boat" to ease after mild inflation data released in late April, and before the renewed weakness of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  For now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to remain on hold at least until the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilises, and until the picture of the domestic economy becomes clearer. \par \par \uc2  ((Sydney newsroom 61-2 9373-1800, sydney.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5j04vyf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
