\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr Stronger Late; RBA Buying, Suharto Comments \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 1998\par 09:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The Australian dollar was stronger late Tuesday, supported by Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and to a less extent, news that Indonesian president Suharto wasn't planning to seek reelection at that country's next round of elections. \par \par \uc2  After sliding to a new near 12-year low of US$0.6173 overnight, the Australian dollar rallied to US$0.6275 in early trading Tuesday after the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the move catching traders by surprise. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have bought Australian dollars near US$0.6200 and again around US$0.6250/US$0.6260 through brokers. Most dealers said they didn't know how much the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought, although one dealer suggested it was in the order of A$200.0 million and A$300.0 million. \par \par \uc2  The last time the Reserve Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market was in early January, when the Australian dollar slipped to a then near-12-year low of US$0.6315, amid panic-selling fueled by the Indonesian rupiah's slide. \par \par \uc2  At 0650 GMT, the Australian dollar was changing hands at US$0.6255, up from US$0.6229 locally late Monday. It also was at Y84.92, above Y84.21 Monday, while the trade-weighted index rose to 57.6 from 57.4. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -led rally was cut short by strong U.S.-based selling interest around US$0.6270 Tuesday, according to dealers. Indeed, U.S. selling has dominated over the last week, prompting some rumors in the market that financier George Soros might be behind the U.S.-based selling, a couple of dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At midafternoon, the Australian dollar staged a modest lift on news that Suharto announced plans to end his 32-year authoritarian rule, saying he would step down after introducing long called-for political reforms and staging parliamentary elections. He didn't give a date for the elections, but said they should be held "as soon as possible." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 19-05-98 \par \par \uc2  0709GMT \par \par \uc2  1054 22766 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5j048oo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
