\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr Stronger Midmorning After RBA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 355 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 1998\par 02:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The Australian dollar remains stronger midmorning Tuesday, after the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 0005 GMT, the Australian dollar is quoted at US$0.6259, up from US$0.6229 locally late Monday. It also is at Y85.10, above Y84.21 Monday, while the trade-weighted index rose has risen to 57.8 from 57.4. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar fell to a new near 12-year low overnight at US$0.6173, according to one domestic bank dealer, as the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fronted a stronger U.S. dollar versus the yen, weaker commodity and base metal prices, and ongoing instability in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  However, the Australian dollar changed direction in early New Zealand trading Tuesday after the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In response, the Australian dollar rallied around one U.S. cent to touch a US$0.6275 high in early Sydney trading. \par \par \uc2  A couple of dealers say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is thought to have bought through the brokers at levels around US$0.6200 and US$0.6250/US$0.6260, although one dealer says the Reserve Bank bought as low as US$0.6180. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say it is difficult to determine the amount involved, although one European bank dealer believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought around A$200.0 million to A$300.0 million dollars. \par \par \uc2  The European Bank dealer suggests that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed at providing "a circuit breaker" to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent slump. He adds that "by going through the brokers, that's publicizing their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Dealer comment that there is currently good U.S. name selling around US$0.6270 which is making it difficult for the Australian dollar to rally. A couple of dealers add that there are rumors U.S. investor George Soros might be behind the U.S.-based selling which has weighed on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last week. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 19-05-98 \par \par \uc2  0026GMT \par \par \uc2  1054 22766 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5j047fi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
