\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr -2: Seen Under Pressure Over Coming Months \b0\par\par\uc2 361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 1998\par 08:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Standard & Poor's managing director in Australia Graham Lee, said Wednesday that a weaker Australian dollar might be a positive factor for Australia. \par \par \uc2  "A weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does obviously provide encouragement to exports and in the current environment that would be a positive factor for Australia," Lee commented in a radio interview. He said the ratings agency doesn't expect the Asian slowdown will effect Australia's credit rating, although uncertainty surrounding Asia will continue for some time. Lee also remarked that Standard & Poor's wasn't too concerned about a forecast widening of Australia's current-account deficit in 1998. \par \par \uc2  A National Australia Bank dealer said "the market is struggling to move one way or the other" currently, while traders await clearer direction on the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following its dip to near 12-year lows overnight. \par \par \uc2  The dealer suggested that while the Australian dollar has edged under its US$0.6315 January low, the level reached just before the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants aren't too concerned about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage. Most analysts contend that as long as the Australian dollar's slide is orderly and in response to fundamental issues, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to step in to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, an analyst at Westpac Banking Corp. in a report Wednesday cautioned that "current account deterioration, fears of a 'second wave' Asian financial crisis and threats to the commodity price outlook" will weigh on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over coming months. \par \par \uc2  Citibank Ltd. economists Stephen Koukoulas and Annette Beacher commented after the housing finance data Wednesday that "soft data and the increased prospects for a monetary policy easing will continue to limit any upside (on the Australian dollar), and could actually spark further weakness over the near term." \par \par \uc2  According to Citibank, "A strong U.S. dollar could further excite Australian dollar bears and see the Australian dollar move towards US$0.6100 in the near term." \par \par \uc2  -By Sandra Muldoon; 612-9290-8871; smuldoon@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 13-05-98 \par \par \uc2  0655GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5d03zz9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
