\par\par \b \uc2  WEEK AHEAD-Markets to watch local budget/Indonesia. \b0\par\par\uc2 946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, May 11 (Reuters) - The Australian budget and Indonesian riots will be the focus for local finance and equity markets this week, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Economists expect the 1998/99 budget to be handed down by Treasurer Peter Costello at 7.30 p.m. on Tuesday to meet the government's surplus target but fall short of official hopes for economic growth in next 12 months. \par \par \uc2  They said the government's prognosis for Asia and its impact on the local economy will be keenly watched by debt markets and overseas-based traders on Tuesday night, as will any clues about the tax reform package likely to be unveiled in the next few months by Prime Minister John Howard and the sale of the remaining two-thirds of Teltra. \par \par \uc2  "This year it will be framed against the uncertain impact on Australia of the Asian crisis and a looming federal election," ANZ Investment Bank said of the budget. \par \par \uc2  "However, rather than being a typical election budget, we expect it to focus on fiscal restraint, with plenty of rhetoric about fiscal consolidation and responsibility. \par \par \uc2  "In truth though, the government will not be looking to lock in budget surpluses to provide scope for pre-election policy announcements later on," ANZ said. \par \par \uc2  Australia's dollar remained under 64 U.S. cents in New York trade on Friday and was likely headed lower with the budget to again have little or no impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers and investors. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said a repeat of last week's riots in Indonesia could spook the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and threaten to match January's 63.16 cent low, and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  sends 60 percent of its exports to Asia and the riots have revived speculation that the Asian economic and social crisis, which appeared to have abated with IMF packages, could be about to reignite. \par \par \uc2  A manageable rise in U.S. non-farm payrolls last Friday has led some traders to speculate that the Fed won't impose a near-term rate hike, a topic which has preoccupied markets in the past week and pushed Wall Street lower and yields higher. \par \par \uc2  AUSTRALIAN BONDS (Fri 10-yr close 5.96, steady) \par \par \uc2  The market will start the week with a steady tone after U.S. Treauries finished lower in Friday trading due to profit-taking, and following a surprise fall in April unemployment which initially sparked inflation fears. \par \par \uc2  This week's budget will mark the end of an eventful three-week calendar which has seen contained inflation and wages reports, deteriorating trade data and a clear signal from the Reserve Bank that the board has an easing bias. \par \par \uc2  However, the budget has not really gripped the imagination of the bond market, as it is expected that Treasurer Peter Costello will remain fiscally prudent. The key issues over tax reform and the remaining sale of Telstra are likely to be left to another day. \par \par \uc2  The government's sale of its remaining two-thirds stake in Telstra has been well flagged and the proceeds have already been pledged to retire debt, but it is contingent on the government retaining power at the next election. \par \par \uc2  Those hoping that the budget papers may map out what the bond market structure will be after the Telstra sale wipes some some 40 percent off outstanding debt may be disappointed as it will be more likely in the Treasury's finance management document in a couple of months time. \par \par \uc2  AUSTRALIAN DOLLAR (Fri close $US0.6369, NYK 0.6348) \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar will likely head lower this week, with the neutral impact of the budget to be overwhelmed by concerns that student rioting in Indonesia could easily worse. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is inextricably linked to the social and economic woes of Southeast Asia, although the rationalisations are sometimes tenous. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply last week in response to the riots and signs that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has an easing bias. \par \par \uc2  One trader said the Australian dollar, yen, and the New Zealand dollar would be hardest hit in the region if the Asian contagion flared again as some suspect it will. \par \par \uc2  AUSTRALIAN STOCKS (Fri close All Ords 2780.5, -0.3) \par \par \uc2  The Australian equity market will start the week with a firmer tone after Wall Street closed higher due to speculation that the Fed will not hike rates anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  The Dow ended 78.47 points higher at 9,055.15. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment there doesn't seem to be many days where Wall Street follows a trend," said Reynolds and Co dealer Grant Williams, noting the U.S. market's recent retreat into a range. \par \par \uc2  "People are having another look at the U.S.," agreed a dealer at an American-owned brokerage. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like a market that is getting ahead of itself." \par \par \uc2  Locally, the federal budget due this week has kept some people out of the market althogh any buildup has been low key with an election widely expected before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  "There hasn't been much talk about the budget and I think there is a lack of interest," said Reynold's Williams. \par \par \uc2  "You would expect them to be sensible with the budget and save the goodies for the election campaign, and that is one reason why the market has been pretty ho-hum." \par \par \uc2  In terms of set pieces this week, Tuesday's budget takes top billing while for corporates Tuesday also sees regional bank St George Bank report half year results, while building materials group James Hardie reports its full year numbers on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Sydney newsroom 61-2 9373-1800 sydney.newsroom. \par \par \uc2  reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5a05pwm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
