\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP/Australian Dollar -3: Mkt Wary Of  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 279 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1998\par 09:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in its quarterly report on the economy released Thursday, said Australian dollar trading since it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in January 'has remained orderly and the Bank has not seen a need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' \par \par \uc2  David de Garis, senior economist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and New Zealand Banking Group Ltd., suggested that the Australian dollar would need to slip a further 0.5 to 1.0 U.S. cent from current levels before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might consider stepping in to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  That might happen as Richard Grace, economist at Colonial State Bank, noted that 'commodity prices and the current account deficit will continue to weigh on the Australian dollar' over coming months. \par \par \uc2  Data released Monday showed Australia recorded a trade deficit in March of A$1.33 billion, the largest monthly deficit on record. The result pushed concern about a deterioration of Australia's current account deficit in 1998 to the forefront of traders' minds. \par \par \uc2  Demand for Australian exports is seen damped by slower growth in the Asian region in 1998 following Southeast Asia's economic turmoil. Around 15% of Australia's exports make their way to the Southeast Asian region, although this percentage jumps toward 60% if the northern economies, like Japan, are included. Meanwhile, relatively robust domestic growth is seen supporting Australia's demand for imports. \par \par \uc2  The current account deficit stood at 4.3% of gross domestic product in the fourth quarter of 1997. Some analysts have forecast the deficit might widen to 6.0% of GDP in the fiscal year ending June 30, 1999. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 07-05-98 \par \par \uc2  0732GMT \par \par \uc2  (CORRECTED 0853GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5703uay\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
