\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP/Australian Dollar -2: Trade Deterioration Eyed \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1998\par 09:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar saw a steady decline from a US$0.8003 peak in mid-March 1997 to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's low in early January 1998 of US$0.6315. In particular, fueling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide from mid-1997 onwards was concern over the impact of Southeast Asia's economic woes on the Australian economy. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in early January to stem the Australian dollar's slide, which saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subsequently rebound to US$0.6880 in early February as battered Southeast Asian economies showed signs of stabilizing. \par \par \uc2  However, recent weeks have seen the Australian dollar under renewed pressure, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  touching a US$0.6344 low Thursday, its lowest level reached since January. \par \par \uc2  Traders no doubt will be on alert for any sign of the Reserve Bank of Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on approaches to US$0.6315, analysts said, given that this is the level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last stepped in to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A break below this level will see the Australian dollar reach levels not touched since 1986. \par \par \uc2  But while the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it fell to US$0.6315 in January, there is little reason to suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would again defend this level, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  'If the (Australian dollar's) depreciation was orderly through (US$0.6315), there's no reason why the Reserve Bank of Australia would come into the market again,' said Societe Generale's Maguire. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, given weakness in Asia and emerging evidence of the damping impact of slower Asian growth on Australia's exports, 'the Reserve Bank of Australia might be comfortable with the Australian dollar depreciating a bit further' from current levels, Maguire said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 07-05-98 \par \par \uc2  0728GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du5703uaf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
