\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Indonesia violence spooks Asian markets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Bradley Perrett \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1998\par 05:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 7 (Reuters) - Rioting in Indonesia spooked Asian markets on Thursday, sparking a general slide in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and share prices. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were also unnerved by a Chinese central banker's musings about pressure to devalue the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the Tokyo share market fell on the seemingly never-ending worries over the Japanese economy. The Nikkei average finished morning trading at 15,158.30, down 0.56 percent. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's rupiah fell to around 10,000 per dollar in early trading, down about 10 percent from Wednesday's close, although it later recovered most of that loss. \par \par \uc2  Fears about the unrest in Indonesia also weakened the Thai baht. It was quoted at 39.75/39.95 per dollar against 38.70/38.80 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The baht's drop was due mainly to rioting in Indonesia, which drove all regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down," said a senior dealer at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  "There were no domestic factors at all, it was purely regional," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar, reliant on Asian trade, slumped to 63.40/45 U.S. cents from 64.05/10. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see any hope for now," said one dealer in Jakarta. "People are very nervous as to whether the government has control over the whole situation." \par \par \uc2  Singapore's benchmark share index fell through key support at 1,400 points on Thursday morning following news of the Indonesian riots. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares were also dragged lower. The blue chip index fell to 9,874 down 2.3 percent in morning trade. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit dropped through 4.000 per dollar, before recovering to 3.9500/9800 by 0200 GMT, still down 2.3 percent on Wednesday, and the Kuala Lumpur share index quiceven saw reasons to sell in merger talks between auto giants Daimler-Benz AG  and Chrysler Corp . Elsewhere in the world, the prospects of further mergers has led carmaker's shares higher. \par \par \uc2  Another factor undermining Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values was a report in which the chief economist of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  predicted growing pressure for devaluation of the yuan in the second half of this year. \par \par \uc2  The solid refusal of China to countenance devaluation, despite increasingly cheap and therefore competitive rival \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has been one of the few factors underpinning Asian markets since they began to collapse in July. \par \par \uc2  In an academic paper presented earlier this week and reproduced on Thursday on the website of The Australian Financial Review newspaper, chief economist Yi Gang said the government would honour its pledge to keep the yuan at its current level of 8.27 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "But pressure for devaluation will increase in the second half of 1998 due to the impact of the (Asian) financial crisis on China's exports," Yi said, advocating easier monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said this revived talk of a whole new round of competitive depreciations in the Asian region. \par \par \uc2  The report was bad news for the Australian dollar, too, since 60 percent of Australia's exports are bound for Asia. \par \par \uc2  Although some sellers fear the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the Aussie dollar's slide, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suffering so much negative news that bears are now spoiling for a showdown with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a push to 60 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  "In the near term, I'm not sure what's going to give us a boost out of this negative sentiment," said Joanne Masters, international economist at Macquarie Bank. \par \par \uc2  In late morning trading the Thai baht was bid at 39.600 per dollar, down 2.4 percent, and the Philippine peso at 40.450 per dollar, down 2.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5705xdk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
