\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Australian markets dive on Asian woes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ian Pemberton \par \uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 1997\par 08:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Oct 27 (Reuter) - Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets plunged on Monday as the nation found itself in the middle of the Southeast Asian financial crisis, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar ended at a 46-month low of 68.67 U.S. cents and could slide to as low as 64 cents before any positive sentiment took hold, they said. \par \par \uc2  "It's a selling fever -- there's billions changing hands," said one dealer of the day's frantic trade. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen some support at 68.40 U.S. cents, but really, we're all waiting to see if New York drops the hammer." \par \par \uc2  There was talk in the morning that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was checking prices, often a prelude to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not go any further. \par \par \uc2  Treasurer Peter Costello also took a hands-off approach to the Australian dollar's free-fall, noting that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were linked to a variety of factors. \par \par \uc2  "In relation to the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the government operates a policy of open trading and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the ability to adjust in relation to external events," Costello told parliament. \par \par \uc2  "The policy has been to allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to find its right level, and it's been quite stable over the course of the last 18 months," Costello said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slump reflected offshore concerns that the persistent financial and economic woes hitting Southeast Asia would spread to North Asia, and hence to Australia's main export destinations. \par \par \uc2  But if these were not enough, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sent reeling late on Friday on news that the Swiss were considering selling about half their gold reserves, or 1,400 tonnes, sending the bullion price US$16 lower in New York. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss statement and an associated price plunge took a heavy toll on Australian gold mining shares on Monday, which led the overall share market 3.3 percent lower on the day to 2,477.0, a drop of 84.3 points. \par \par \uc2  This was the biggest fall since March 11, 1996. \par \par \uc2  "It's been a disappointing day. There are a few after-shocks of the events in Asia," said Geoff Sargent, a private client advisor with Colonial Stockbroking. \par \par \uc2  The gold sector lost 11.3 percent, with miners who had not hedged their future production being hardest hit. \par \par \uc2  "You've got panic selling basically and you'd have to suspect that the gold price has got further to go," one senior gold analyst said. \par \par \uc2  Australia is the world's third largest gold producer behind South Africa and the United States, with expected 1997 production of 300 tonnes. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtar03lbg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
